{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"ls -a; node -v; rg -n 'theme|colors|Shadow|shadow|browser|memory|canvas|schem|gap|font-family|--axylo-' docs/02-product-spec.md\"","aggregated_output":".\n..\n.agents\n.aws\n.codex\n.git\n.gitignore\nAGENTS.md\nREADME.md\nSTATUS.md\nacceptance\nbench\ndocs\nnode_modules\npackage-lock.json\npackage.json\npackages\nresearch\nreviews\nscripts\ntsconfig.base.json\nvitest.config.ts\nv24.21.0\n9:**Axylo is the everyday chart you can ship without checking it.** The smallest correct call produces a chart that represents the data faithfully, stays readable at any width and in any color scheme, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to the developer and to the reader, instead of drawing a guess.\n39:| Correct before configurable | A default that can mislead (truncated bars, interpolated gaps, a silently dropped label) is a bug, even if an option fixes it. |\n43:| One way to do each thing | One logical data model, one method per kind of change, one place for colors (CSS). |\n44:| The page is the theme | Font, text color and color scheme come from the page. |\n52:| Line | Multi-series, gaps; time, date, numeric or category x | How did it change? | 1.0 |\n70:| W2 | Fill a dashboard | Dozens of charts per page; container-sized; themed by the page; loading, empty and error states; a chart can take its name from the card's own heading. |\n95:| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n98:| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n111:| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to two lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n112:| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n114:| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n126:| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n141:| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n171:| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n204:| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n213:Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n224:| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n260:| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\n267:| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\n270:| Colors and fonts from CSS | R10. | Kept. Repaint triggers specified; `palette` option replaced by `colors` keyed by series name. |\n293:| Canvas or SVG paths for marks? | Decide from the M1 comparison: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, theme toggle, forced colors, print. | M1 record. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
